# Chapter 7 · Laws, Policies and Government Initiatives against Violence

> "**Government initiatives for combating violence against women**" was a short note in ==2018==. The ==2024== question asked: "What forms of gender-based violence are given in the **Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016**?" A 2026 MCQ asked for the ==2016== honour-killing law.

## 7.1  The constitutional and international framework

- **Constitution of 1973:** **Article 9** (security of person), **Article 14** (dignity of man and privacy of home), **Article 25** (equality; special provision for women), **Article 35** (protection of the family, the mother and the child), **Article 37** (just and humane conditions).
- **International commitments:** **CEDAW** (acceded ==1996==) and its General Recommendations 19 and 35; the **Beijing Platform** (Area D); the **ICCPR** (==2010==) and the **Convention against Torture** (==2010==); **SDG targets 5.2 and 5.3**; the **EU GSP+** status (==2014==), which requires effective implementation of 27 conventions.

## 7.2  The legislative timeline

::: fig timeline | Pakistan's main laws against violence against women.
@layout vertical
1860: Pakistan Penal Code (assault, murder, s. 509 insult to modesty)
1976: Dowry and Bridal Gifts (Restriction) Act
1979: Hudood Ordinances — Zina Ordinance endangers rape victims
2004: Criminal Law (Amendment) Act — honour killing defined
2006: Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act — rape back in the PPC
2010: Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act; s. 509 PPC amended
2011: Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act; Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act
2013: Sindh Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act; Sindh Child Marriages Restraint Act (18)
2014: Balochistan Domestic Violence Act
2016: Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act; honour-killing law; anti-rape amendment; PECA
2018: Trafficking in Persons Act; Transgender Persons Act
2020: Zainab Alert Act; Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act
2021: Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act; KP Domestic Violence Act
2022: Workplace harassment law amended
2025: Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act (18); Islamabad Domestic Violence Act
:::

### Key laws explained

1. **Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act 2006** — moved rape from the **Zina Ordinance** back into the **Penal Code**, ended the requirement of four witnesses for rape, and stopped the conversion of rape complaints into adultery charges (MCQ 2016).
2. **Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act 2010** — requires every organisation to have an **inquiry committee** and a **code of conduct**, and created federal and provincial **Ombudspersons**; amended in ==2022== to widen "workplace" and "employee".
3. **Section 509 PPC (2010)** — harassment in public places, punishable by up to three years' imprisonment and a fine.
4. **Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011** — sections 336A and 336B PPC: 14 years to life imprisonment and a minimum fine of one million rupees.
5. **Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act 2011** — criminalised *vani* and *swara* (s. 310A), denial of inheritance (s. 498A), forced marriage (s. 498B) and marriage to the Qur'an (s. 498C).
6. **Criminal Law (Amendment) (Offences in the name or pretext of Honour) Act 2016** — mandatory life imprisonment even where heirs forgive.
7. **Criminal Law (Amendment) (Offences relating to Rape) Act 2016** — DNA testing, in-camera trials, and penalties for police who obstruct investigation.
8. **Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act 2016** — online harassment, stalking and image-based abuse.
9. **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021** — **Special Sexual Offences Investigation Units** in districts, **Anti-Rape Crisis Cells**, special courts, in-camera trials, decisions preferably within **four months**, and a sex-offenders register.

## 7.3  The Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016 (2024)

The **Punjab Assembly** passed the Act on ==24 February 2016==; it was published on ==29 February 2016==. Its aim, in its own words, is "to establish an effective system of **protection, relief and rehabilitation** of women against violence". It is mainly a **civil protection** law, not a penal code — it gives women remedies and services.

### (1) The forms of violence it names — section 2(r)

::: table | Forms of violence under the Punjab Act 2016, section 2(r).
@widths 3.6,10.4
Form | Meaning under the Act, with an example
Abetment of an offence | Helping or encouraging an offence against a woman — e.g. in-laws who incite a husband
Domestic violence | Violence by a person with whom the woman lives or has lived in a house, related by blood, marriage or adoption — e.g. beating by a husband or brother
Sexual violence | Sexual assault, including within the household
Psychological abuse | Oppressive behaviour or restriction of movement causing psychological deterioration — e.g. depression or attempted suicide, certified by a panel of psychologists
Economic abuse | Denial of food, clothing and shelter according to the defendant's income, or taking away the woman's income without consent
Stalking | Following, watching or contacting a woman persistently so as to cause fear
Cybercrime | Offences committed through electronic means — e.g. harassment or blackmail online
:::

### (2) The remedies — court orders

A complaint goes to the **Family Court**, which must fix the first hearing within **seven days** and decide within **90 days**.

::: fig grid | The orders a court can pass under the Punjab Act 2016.
@cols 2
Interim order (s. 6): immediate protection while the case is pending
Protection order (s. 7): no contact, stay away, move out, surrender weapons, GPS bracelet for grave violence
Residence order (s. 8): she may not be evicted; may stay in the house or a shelter; he pays rent for other accommodation
Monetary order (s. 9): compensation for economic abuse, lost earnings, medical costs, damage, maintenance
:::

The protection order's most famous feature was the **GPS ankle or wrist bracelet** for a defendant who commits or threatens "grave violence". Critics called it un-Islamic and anti-family; it was rarely used in practice.

### (3) The protection system

- **Universal toll-free helpline** (s. 3).
- **District Women Protection Committees** (s. 11), led by the district administration, with health, police, social welfare and prosecution officers and civil-society members.
- **Protection Centres** — "**Violence against Women Centres**" (s. 13): one-stop centres for police reporting, FIR registration, medical examination, forensic evidence within 24 hours, legal aid, counselling and mediation. The first opened in **Multan** in ==2017==.
- **Shelter homes** in each district (s. 13).
- **Women Protection Officers** (s. 14), who can enter premises (with notice) to rescue a woman.

### (4) Penalties

- **Breach of an order** or tampering with the GPS tracker: up to **one year** and a fine of Rs 50,000–200,000; repeat breach: **one to two years** (s. 20).
- **False complaint:** up to **three months** or a fine of Rs 50,000–100,000 (s. 19).

### (5) Reaction and assessment

::: balance | The Punjab Act 2016 — praise and criticism
- **Praise:** the first law in Pakistan to recognise **economic and psychological abuse**, **stalking** and **cybercrime** as violence; a fast-track civil remedy; one-stop centres that bring police, doctors and lawyers under one roof; a model for other provinces.
- **Criticism:** religious parties and the **Council of Islamic Ideology** called it "un-Islamic" and a threat to the family; mediation and reconciliation provisions can push women back into abusive homes; implementation was slow — few centres beyond Multan, limited staff and funds; low awareness among women.
-> **Line to take:** a **progressive** law in design, whose impact depends on **implementation** — centres in every district, trained staff and public awareness.
:::

## 7.4  Other provincial laws

- **Sindh Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act 2013** — the first provincial DV law; protection orders and penalties.
- **Balochistan Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act 2014**.
- **Khyber Pakhtunkhwa Domestic Violence against Women (Prevention and Protection) Act 2021** — after years of opposition.
- **Islamabad (ICT) Domestic Violence (Prevention and Protection) Act 2025** — moved by **Sharmila Farooqui**; passed by the National Assembly (13 November) and the Senate (28 November ==2025==).

## 7.5  Government initiatives and institutions (2018)

::: fig grid | Government initiatives against violence against women in Pakistan.
@cols 3
Commissions: NCSW (2000; Act 2012); provincial commissions; NCHR (2012)
Ombudspersons: federal and provincial harassment ombudspersons (2010)
Police: women's police stations (1994); gender desks; virtual women police station; Special Sexual Offences Investigation Units (2021)
One-stop centres: Violence against Women Centre, Multan (2017); Anti-Rape Crisis Cells; Women Protection Centres
Shelters: Dar-ul-Aman shelters; Shaheed Benazir Bhutto Centres for Women
Helplines: Punjab Women Helpline 1043; human-rights helpline 1099; police 15
Courts: family courts; gender-based violence courts; special courts under the 2021 Act
Policy: National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women (2002); provincial women's empowerment policies
Data and awareness: Punjab Women Protection Authority (2017); campaigns; 16 Days of Activism
:::

1. **National Commission on the Status of Women (NCSW)** — set up in ==2000==, made statutory by the **NCSW Act 2012**; reviews laws and policies and recommends reforms.
2. **Women's police stations** — first established in ==1994== under Benazir Bhutto; today supplemented by **gender protection units** and women's help desks in police stations.
3. **Shelters** — ***Dar-ul-Aman*** homes run by social welfare departments, and crisis centres for women.
4. **Helplines** — the **Punjab Women Helpline 1043**, the federal **1099** human-rights helpline, and police emergency **15**.
5. **Violence against Women Centre, Multan** (==2017==) — the first one-stop centre under the Punjab Act.
6. **Punjab Women Protection Authority** (==2017==) — to run the protection system.
7. **Courts** — special gender-based violence courts piloted in Punjab from ==2017==, and special courts under the **Anti-Rape Act 2021**; Sindh reported a rise in conviction rates in districts with such courts.
8. **Ombudspersons** for workplace harassment and for women's property rights (==2020==).
9. **Social protection** — the **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==), paid to women, which some studies link to greater bargaining power at home.

## 7.6  The enforcement gap

::: fig cycle | Why laws have not stopped violence — the enforcement gap.
@centre Laws on paper, violence in practice
Low awareness: women do not know the laws
Social pressure: families force settlements
Police refuse or delay FIRs; poor investigation
Slow courts; hostile witnesses; weak forensics
Conviction rates about 0.5% (SSDO 2024)
Impunity encourages repeat offences
:::

The main problem is not a lack of laws but their **implementation**: low awareness, police attitudes, few women police, poor forensic capacity, slow courts, compromise and pardons, too few shelters, and uneven provincial capacity after the **18th Amendment** (==2010==).

::: eye | The model structure for the 2018 note
1. Framework (Constitution, CEDAW, SDG 5.2). 2. Laws (2006, 2010, 2011, 2016, 2021). 3. Institutions (NCSW, women's police stations, ombudspersons). 4. Services (VAWC Multan 2017, 1043 helpline, shelters). 5. Courts and investigation units. 6. Assessment — the enforcement gap. About 600 words; draw the grid.
:::
